cm: x=5 is sh's - var x 5 makes a variable, set x 5 changes one, $env set X 5 the environment (line 1)
cm: $1 is sh's - the script's arguments are $args, $args[0] the first (line 1)
cm: for name values... { } - no in, as sh has (line 1)
cm: name() { } is sh's - var name {|params| ... } makes a function (line 1)
cm: if condition { ... } - a block, not sh's then and fi (line 1)
cm: export is sh's - $env set NAME value (line 1)
cm: local is sh's - var name value - a function's variables are its own (line 1)
cm: unset is sh's - $m del key, $env del NAME (line 1)
cm: function is sh's - var name {|params| ... } (line 1)
cm: `...` is sh's - $( ) captures a command's output (line 1)
cm: fi is sh's - blocks: if cond { }, while cond { }, for x values { }, case v { } (line 1)
cm: nosuchprog: no such program (line 1)
still here
cm: echo -n and -e are sh's - echo prints its words and a newline; printf '%s' text prints none (line 1)
cm: @( ) is not taken in a double-quoted text or a here-document - $( ) is the text there, \@( the characters (line 1)
mail ada@(home)
cm: read takes one name - it reads a line as it is: no -r (line 1)
cm: $? is sh's - the last command's status is $status (line 1)
cm: $? is sh's - the last command's status is $status (line 1)
cm: $# is sh's - $($args count) counts the arguments (line 1)
cm: $@ and $* are sh's - @args spreads the arguments, $args is their list (line 1)
cm: $! is sh's - $job is the job & started last (line 1)
cm: <<< is bash's - echo text | cmd, or << END and the lines (line 1)
